  "lean": {
    "statement": "∀ {E : Type u} [inst : NormedAddCommGroup E] [inst_1 : InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [inst_2 : FiniteDimensional ℝ E]\n  [inst_3 : MeasurableSpace E] [inst_4 : BorelSpace E] {V : E → ℝ} {α β : NNReal},\n  0 < α →\n    α ≤ β →\n      ContDiff ℝ 2 V →\n        (∀ (x w : E), ↑α * ‖w‖ ^ 2 ≤ ((fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x) w) w ∧ ((fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ V) x) w) w ≤ ↑β * ‖w‖ ^ 2) →\n          0 < ↑(Module.finrank ℝ E) →\n            ∀ (η τ : NNReal × E × E × ℕ × (ℕ → E) → ℝ),\n              Measurable η →\n                Measurable τ →\n                  (∀ (s : NNReal × E × E × ℕ × (ℕ → E)), 0 < η s) →\n                    (∀ (s : NNReal × E × E × ℕ × (ℕ → E)), 0 < τ s) →\n                      ∀ (M : ProbabilityTheory.Kernel (NNReal × E × E × ℕ × (ℕ → E)) E)\n                        [ProbabilityTheory.IsMarkovKernel M] (threshold : NNReal),\n                        have d := ↑(Module.finrank ℝ E);\n                        have v := fun s => (η s + τ s) / (↑β + ↑s.1);\n                        have bp := fun s => s.1 + (v s)⁻¹.toNNReal;\n                        have obs := fun p => p.2.1 + √(τ p.1 / (↑β + ↑p.1.1)) • p.2.2;\n                        have center := fun p => (↑(bp p.1))⁻¹ • (↑p.1.1 • p.1.2.1 + (v p.1)⁻¹ • obs p);\n                        have F := fun p x => V x + ↑(bp p.1) / 2 * ‖x - center p‖ ^ 2;\n                        have T := fun p x => x - (↑β + ↑(bp p.1))⁻¹ • gradient (F p) x;\n                        have q := fun n p => ‖gradient (F p) ((T p)^[n] p.2.1)‖ ^ 2;\n                        have N := fun s => if h : ∃ n, q n s ≤ (fun p => (↑α + ↑(bp p.1)) * d) s then Nat.find h else 0;\n                        have out := fun p => (T p)^[N p] p.2.1;\n                        have update := fun p =>\n                          (bp p.1, center p, out p, p.1.2.2.2.1 + 1, fun n => Nat.casesOn n (obs p) p.1.2.2.2.2);\n                        have μ := MeasureTheory.MeasureSpace.volume.tilted fun x => -V x;\n                        MeasureTheory.IsProbabilityMeasure μ ∧\n                          ∃ T H P,\n                            ∃ (_ : ProbabilityTheory.IsMarkovKernel T) (_ : ProbabilityTheory.IsMarkovKernel H) (_ :\n                              ProbabilityTheory.IsMarkovKernel P),\n                              (∀ (s : NNReal × E × E × ℕ × (ℕ → E)),\n                                  T s = μ.tilted fun x => -(↑s.1 / 2) * ‖x - s.2.1‖ ^ 2) ∧\n                                (∀ (s : NNReal × E × E × ℕ × (ℕ → E)),\n                                    H s =\n                                      MeasureTheory.Measure.map (fun p => p.1 + p.2)\n                                        ((T s).prod\n                                          (MeasureTheory.Measure.map (fun z => √(v s) • z)\n                                            (ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E)))) ∧\n                                  (∀ (s : NNReal × E × E × ℕ × (ℕ → E)),\n                                      P s =\n                                        if threshold ≤ s.1 then MeasureTheory.Measure.dirac s\n                                        else\n                                          MeasureTheory.Measure.map (fun z => update (s, z))\n                                            ((M s).prod (ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E))) ∧\n                                    have Q := fun s =>\n                                      MeasureTheory.Measure.map (fun p => p.1 + p.2)\n                                        ((M s).prod\n                                          (MeasureTheory.Measure.map (fun z => √(τ s / (↑β + ↑s.1)) • z)\n                                            (ProbabilityTheory.stdGaussian E)));\n                                    have e := fun s =>\n                                      if threshold ≤ s.1 then 0 else InformationTheory.klDiv (Q s) (H s);\n                                    Measurable e ∧\n                                      ∀ (R : ProbabilityTheory.Kernel (NNReal × E × E × ℕ × (ℕ → E)) E)\n                                        [ProbabilityTheory.IsMarkovKernel R],\n                                        (Measurable fun s => InformationTheory.klDiv (R s) (T s)) ∧\n                                          ∀ (s : NNReal × E × E × ℕ × (ℕ → E)),\n                                            InformationTheory.klDiv ((R.comp P) s) (T s) ≤\n                                              e s +\n                                                ∫⁻ (t : NNReal × E × E × ℕ × (ℕ → E)),\n                                                  InformationTheory.klDiv (R t) (T t) ∂P s",
    "statement_sha256": "ef8fe6c5207e6d86a83543fe5f303ea66d06073a0b5762f90517c2b7607e3626",
    "compiled": true,
    "approved_definition_context": [
      "NNReal is the type of nonnegative real numbers. Real.toNNReal truncates a real below at zero. Module.finrank is finite real vector-space dimension. fderiv is the Frechet derivative; gradient is the real inner-product gradient. Function.iterate applies its function the displayed natural number of times. Nat.find is the least natural satisfying the displayed existential predicate. Nat.casesOn defines a sequence by its zeroth value and shifted tail.",
      "ProbabilityTheory.Kernel is a jointly measurable transition interface; IsMarkovKernel says every input fiber is a probability measure. Kernel.comp R P first draws a state from P then an output from R. InformationTheory.klDiv is extended nonnegative relative entropy. Displayed nonnegative integrals take values in extended nonnegative reals,including infinity. No finiteness or absolute-continuity premise is implicit in the KL notation.",
      "Measure.map is pushforward and Measure.prod is independent product. stdGaussian is standard Gaussian on the displayed finite-dimensional inner-product space. MeasurableSet is with respect to the displayed Borel/measurable structure. The product type is right-associated; Nat-to-E is a full infinite sequence.",
      "Measure.tilted mu f denotes exponential reweighting by exp(f),normalized according to its Lean definition. The definition is total; probability and finite-normalization properties are not inferred from the notation alone. volume on a finite-dimensional real inner-product Borel space denotes its canonical Lebesgue volume."
    ]
  },
